WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.820
- By the way, I was
raised by a black nanny

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:04.680
for my entire life growing up.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:05.782
- Yeah lots of
- Nannies.

00:00:05.782 --> 00:00:06.615
- Yeah, lots of white people

00:00:06.615 --> 00:00:07.530
have been raised by black nannies.

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.338
- I'll try to pivot, I'll try to pivot.

00:00:09.338 --> 00:00:12.630
(Star Spangled Banner plays)

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.600
- Can I ask like what is
everybody's family background here,

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:16.433
in America?

00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:18.330
- I'll start. My dad
is actually indigenous.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.190
He's Native American, Cree Nation,

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:22.380
and my mom is a black American.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:23.730
- Black American as well.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:25.740
I'm born here and my
parents are from Compton.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.930
I also do have indigenous
heritage as well,

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.550
the Houma Nation in Louisiana.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.580
- My parents are just
simple blue collar, working,

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:33.420
white Americans.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.907
- My dad's from California.

00:00:35.907 --> 00:00:39.000
His dad was from Canada,
his mom is from Texas.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.640
- I was born in New York, but
my parents are from Korea.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.040
- I was actually born in
Europe and I've been here

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.670
for seven years and I filed
for a green card recently.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.980
- I was born in Colombia, raised there.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.170
American dad and my
mom is fully Colombian.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.210
- What do we think makes an American,

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.550
or makes someone more American than other?

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:58.200
Like what are our opinions on that?

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.190
- I think it's a choice,

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:02.520
because we're like such a
melting pot of everybody.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.913
But if that makes you feel
proud, then that's what you are.

00:01:05.913 --> 00:01:08.713
- Yeah, I could like agree with that.

00:01:08.713 --> 00:01:10.350
I also think too, though,

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.170
that like lineage does
matter and make a difference.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:14.880
Just 'cause there are
communities in America

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:15.720
that have been here.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.850
I think that should be
taken into consideration.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.790
- That's also my personal
opinion, I get that a lot.

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:22.560
It's like, oh it doesn't get
much more American than you

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.560
but like I haven't served
in the military, I haven't,

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:26.393
you know?

00:01:26.393 --> 00:01:27.290
- Right.

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:29.550
- I've served in the
Air Force for six years

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.880
and I don't think that makes
me more American actually

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.480
because it was more of a
transactional agreement.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:37.680
I got paid to do it.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.900
- Yeah, I agree as well,
I was in the military.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.400
If you're just like a dad who wakes up,

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.500
or a mom who wakes up,
working 80 hours a week,

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:44.700
those are real Americans right there.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:45.960
People are like, "Oh he's military.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.580
You must like love America so much",

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.490
and like America's just a piece
of dirt on the ground to me.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:53.190
People who contribute to their
community in a meaningful way

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:54.510
those are the most American people to me.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:56.220
- [Producer] Can you guys
start rearranging yourselves

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:57.870
as you ask questions?

00:01:57.870 --> 00:01:59.733
- I think number one for sure.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.660
And I just think only
because you two have.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:04.860
- Yeah I can,

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:05.693
- [Lance] Connection to somewhere else.

00:02:05.693 --> 00:02:07.590
- Go in the end for now.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:08.490
- Yeah, I'll move.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.093
- I'm at the bottom honestly.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:15.270
For sure, for so many reasons.

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:18.240
It's kind of weird but I
feel like I'm a human being.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.580
Like that's my metric.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:22.260
That's what I'm concerned
about and I think

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.840
that that's more important
than being from a country.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.400
- I completely agree.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.430
I imagine a future in
which you can go anywhere,

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:31.710
participate in that community respectfully

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:33.060
and like be a part of it.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.310
But I do think it's
important to some degree

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:38.700
to classify things because
there are a lot of wrongs

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.200
that America hasn't righted yet.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.970
I think once we get to
a point where we can all

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:44.640
like kumbaya together,
none of this will matter.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.910
- The fact that there's
so much to talk about

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:51.360
in terms of like what has
gone wrong, or who did what,

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.460
what you're doing is you're
basically trying to blame

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:56.640
somebody, and at all times
blaming people doesn't help

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.270
with the actual issue
is like moving forward.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.070
- But I mean we can't
really move forward until

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.437
we acknowledge what happened
yesterday and we atone for it.

00:03:04.437 --> 00:03:06.390
- But why can't we just be okay

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:09.372
and like just be good like
we're so far beyond that.

00:03:09.372 --> 00:03:10.818
- We have to atone for certain things.

00:03:10.818 --> 00:03:11.693
- No we don't.

00:03:11.693 --> 00:03:14.839
That's like a sort of
like a religious thing.

00:03:14.839 --> 00:03:17.010
- I'll try to pivot, I'll try to pivot.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.260
- You know it's not about pointing fingers

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:20.670
at individual people.

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:23.790
It's pointing fingers at like a system

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.620
that still has like negative effects

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:27.510
for lots of different groups of people.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:29.070
- Does everybody vote that can,

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:30.903
and did you vote since you could?

00:03:30.903 --> 00:03:32.100
- I did not vote.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.267
- I didn't vote, either.

00:03:33.267 --> 00:03:35.010
- I hate politics.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:38.160
- But the main thing
about what we fought for,

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:39.810
on any side in America,

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:42.240
were the right to do
these things like vote.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.640
- But we only have two
parties to pick from.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.320
I would prefer the third party

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:47.670
but the third party doesn't win

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:49.020
and if you vote for the third party,

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.580
you're actually just
hurting your own party.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:51.813
It's rigged.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.020
- It's not really, if you
get involved you can't help

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.030
but be a part, you know,
you could change it.

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:59.130
- I'd rather take care of
my immediate community,

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:00.900
my immediate goals, my immediate life.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:03.240
I'm gonna' be a good
citizen, a good taxpayer.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:05.070
That's all that I need to do.

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:05.903
- I agree with that.

00:04:05.903 --> 00:04:08.850
I also, I can't vote but I
am very politically active.

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:09.683
- Right.

00:04:09.683 --> 00:04:13.200
- So I think that might be
more impactful than like,

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.470
all the, the third of the
population who didn't vote

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:17.370
for whatever reason.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:18.540
- [Producer] Do you guys believe

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:22.440
that citizenship status correlates
to how American you are?

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:23.970
- I don't agree because there's a bunch

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:25.290
of people who come here with,

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:26.700
you have to have a million dollars,

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:29.400
and they put you into like
a community of like-minded,

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:32.640
or like people, and all it
takes is a million dollars

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:33.630
for these people to come in

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:36.780
and they don't have to do
anything but just pay that money.

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:39.240
- Yes, there are people who
have millions of dollars

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:40.530
and invest into the country,

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:42.690
but that's not the majority of the people.

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:44.430
And there are people who are undocumented

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:45.750
and they want to become citizens

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:47.850
because this is the only
country they've ever known.

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:49.140
But they can't.

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:52.500
Are they less American
just because of paperwork.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:54.540
- I think a lot of it has
to do with effort, too.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:55.380
- I thought so.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:56.400
I used to believe that.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.530
I used to think that if I come
here, I go to school here,

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:01.710
I work here, I can become
a citizen eventually.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:03.150
I had to get married

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:05.820
because that was the easiest
way for me to come here.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:07.530
I don't think it's a fair process.

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:09.420
I can get a green card in a year

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:11.190
just 'cause I married an American,

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:13.470
but other people have
been here for decades

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:15.510
and still can't get a green card

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:17.460
and I don't think that
that is fair at all.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:19.230
- I think we should all just go through

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.480
and finish our rankings
and just kind of say why

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:22.313
and then maybe.

00:05:22.313 --> 00:05:23.550
- That would be good.

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:24.720
- I think I'm number one.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:26.130
I think I'm the most American.

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:29.490
I am a continuation of a long line

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:31.290
of people who have done nothing

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:34.530
but thrive and contribute to this country.

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:36.180
- I mean you don't have an America

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:39.180
without black and indigenous
people, just given the history,

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:40.740
and all the contribution.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.900
So I do feel like she
should have been first.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:45.642
- Unfortunately, the thing
about America is that race

00:05:45.642 --> 00:05:48.360
is just connected to everything we do.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:49.650
And that's why it's a mess.

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:51.630
There are several different Americas.

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:53.130
We do not live in the same world.

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:54.800
I can already tell we don't
live in the same world.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:56.070
- By the way, I was raised

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:59.312
by a black nanny for my
entire life growing up.

00:05:59.312 --> 00:06:00.360
- Yeah lots of
- A nanny, so you have.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:01.710
- Lots of white people have
been raised by black nannies.

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:03.060
- Well I'm sorry she raised me

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:04.980
as my mom because my mom wasn't there.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:06.870
- But you don't walk through
life the way that I do,

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:08.490
and I don't walk through
life the way that you do,

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:09.682
or that he does, or that she does.

00:06:09.682 --> 00:06:10.829
- That's such a victim thing to say.

00:06:10.829 --> 00:06:12.353
- I am not a victim.

00:06:12.353 --> 00:06:13.958
- But you have as most
potential as I do at birth.

00:06:13.958 --> 00:06:15.000
- And I know that.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.490
I'm not trying to play the
oppression Olympics here.

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:18.780
I'm just trying to paint a picture

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:20.250
so that you can understand

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:22.890
that not everyone lives life like you.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:24.600
I hope she at least heard what I said

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:26.840
and at least maybe it'll echo, you know,

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:30.270
in the future and maybe she'll
remember like this encounter.

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:33.120
- I heard everybody's
opinion and why they felt

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:35.730
that they did deserve to
be an American or didn't.

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:38.070
We all have our views and I
think they're all gonna' be,

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:40.500
in the end, the same
that we're humans first.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:44.340
- I think it's best for the
sake of this conversation

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:49.320
to try to focus on our individual
reasons why we're American

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:53.610
and not so much because our
ancestors did this and that.

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:55.260
- Then why did you guys
put me at the front.

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:56.580
- Because of what you mentioned,

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:58.740
like as far as I understood,
you want to be here

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:00.690
and this is like where you see yourself

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:03.270
and where you identify yourself as being.

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:04.883
- [Producer] Do you feel American?

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:07.650
- Yeah, it's all I know.

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:08.790
- You're more American than I am

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:10.050
just because you would wanna' stay here.

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:12.360
I would drop my hat and leave immediately.

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:13.980
I'll go to the back line.

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:17.010
If like my girlfriend said
I wanna' go back to Taiwan,

00:07:17.010 --> 00:07:18.210
I'd go with her right away.

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:19.620
Free health care and no mass shootings,

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:22.350
that's already like two
really good pluses to me, so.

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.300
- I choose America all the time.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:27.210
But you know if an like an
alien from outer space came here

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:28.235
and said he loved America

00:07:28.235 --> 00:07:29.610
and he doesn't wanna' go back to space,

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:32.160
he's an American because
he takes pride in it.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:33.450
- I'm gonna' move myself to the.

00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:36.063
- Yeah, I would also rate myself lower.

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:39.030
- I am like a dual citizen.

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:41.730
Like I'm not just American,
I'm also Colombian.

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:44.160
That's still a huge part
of what motivates me,

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:46.950
who I am, the culture that I engage in.

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:48.360
- I think you should come before me

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:51.390
because if we're talking about
like individual contribution,

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:53.400
I think you've done more than I have.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:54.233
- Yeah.

00:07:54.233 --> 00:07:55.410
- There are guys who serve and do things,

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:57.000
and do really great
things for this country,

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.690
but like not everybody in
the military is actively

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:02.340
on the front lines fighting wars.

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:04.410
Not everybody's a Delta
Force Special Operator.

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:06.167
I chose to, not for patriotic reasons,

00:08:06.167 --> 00:08:09.390
'cause like do I go to
school, and get a lot of debt,

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:11.100
and not even know what
I wanna' do with my life

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:12.750
or do I take some time, think about it

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:15.690
and get the government
to pay for my education.

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:17.580
- [Producer] Does everyone feel
comfortable where they are?

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:19.770
- I would say she feels very strongly.

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:20.603
- [Lance] She does.

00:08:20.603 --> 00:08:21.436
- I do.

00:08:21.436 --> 00:08:22.269
- So where would you
wanna' place yourself?

00:08:22.269 --> 00:08:24.510
- I mean, I think that me
personally, the passion

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:27.120
and the importance of me
looking out for everybody

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:29.070
that's in American and wants
to call themself American,

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:30.490
I think that's so valuable.

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:31.699
- I feel like you wanna' say number one.

00:08:31.699 --> 00:08:33.987
- I mean I do, I do, honestly.

00:08:33.987 --> 00:08:36.360
I think that is me.

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.880
- If we're going by her
metric of contributions,

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:41.370
since I am a public servant, I do travel,

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:44.580
I do first responder work,
emergency management.

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:46.680
I would say by that metric
I would be number two.

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:48.543
- You actually help Americans, okay.

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:51.480
- [Producer] This is your final ranking?

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:52.313
- Yes.

00:08:52.313 --> 00:08:53.700
- [Producer] You're locking it in?

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:54.533
Okay, great.

00:08:54.533 --> 00:08:56.070
What I'm gonna' have you guys do

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:58.233
is take a mock citizenship test.

00:08:58.233 --> 00:09:02.612
(group speaks at the same time)

00:09:02.612 --> 00:09:03.445
- We got this.

00:09:03.445 --> 00:09:04.290
- [Producer] You guys have all been given

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:07.530
20 random questions and let's
see how well you guys do.

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:08.848
- Okay.
- Okay.

00:09:08.848 --> 00:09:11.848
(calm upbeat music)

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:21.060
- [Producer] How do you guys
feel like you may have done?

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:22.080
- I think I did bad.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:23.190
- There was at least five questions,

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:24.960
I was like, man, I don't
know the answer to this.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:29.463
- [Producer] In 7th, we
have Abe with 11 out of 20.

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:35.010
We have a tie, Sydney, 11 out of 20.

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:35.843
- A tie?

00:09:35.843 --> 00:09:36.676
- [Producer] You and Abe tied.

00:09:36.676 --> 00:09:39.030
- Oh, okay, sorry Mr. Adler.

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:42.033
- [Producer] In 5th we
have Devin, 12 out of 20.

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:47.700
- Sydney and I are
definitely very impassioned

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:49.470
about the way we feel about America

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:52.680
and we were the lowest
on the totem pole, so.

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:55.173
- [Producer] In 4th Lance, 14 out of 20.

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:01.083
In 3rd Daniel, 18 out of 20.

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:03.600
And at a tie for first,

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:07.172
we have Gabriel and
Lilla with 20 out of 20

00:10:07.172 --> 00:10:09.672
(group claps)

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:15.000
- [Producer] Passing the
civics test is a 12 out of 20.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:16.056
- What'd you get?

00:10:16.056 --> 00:10:17.556
- 11.
- We failed.

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:25.110
- It's kind of funny

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:26.820
that the people who had the loudest voices

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:29.040
are the ones that are at the
bottom of this whole ladder.

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:30.036
- That tends to happen.

00:10:30.036 --> 00:10:30.869
- [Sydney] That's fair.

00:10:30.869 --> 00:10:31.702
- [Devin] Of course.

00:10:31.702 --> 00:10:34.020
I will say when Lilla was
talking about how hard

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:36.420
she had to contribute just
to stay in this country

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:38.070
I think that speaks more volumes

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:39.690
than be being able to take a test.

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:40.523
- A hundred percent.

00:10:40.523 --> 00:10:41.356
- I agree.

00:10:41.356 --> 00:10:45.330
- I was surprised by how
low some of the scores were

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:48.630
but I also wasn't surprised
that I got them all right

00:10:48.630 --> 00:10:50.760
because this is something I have to study.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:54.600
So I don't think it's necessarily fair.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:55.590
- It's much more important

00:10:55.590 --> 00:10:57.810
for them to know what's
going on right now,

00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:00.420
rather than knowing like
random like trivia questions

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:02.340
about history that not even American,

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:04.110
like American born people, know.

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:07.680
Being an American is not easily definable.

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:10.290
There's gonna' be always a
sizable portion of the US

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:12.450
that no matter how American you are,

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:14.190
and how hard you work to be here,

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:17.070
their never gonna' recognize
you as an American.

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:20.280
- I believe in a future in
which America is a place

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:22.050
where everyone can live harmoniously.

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:23.370
I think we just need to do a better job

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:25.740
of listening to each other,
being more empathetic.

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:27.870
- [Producer] Thank you guys so much.

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:29.428
Uh, let's cut.

00:11:29.428 --> 00:11:33.178
(Star Spangled Banner plays)

